WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.247
Validation code deserves the same testing discipline as business logic because it defines what the application will accept.

2
00:00:08.247 --> 00:00:23.808
Unit tests should cover valid examples, but they should also include negative tests for missing fields, malformed values, unexpected types, overlong strings, empty arrays, duplicate items, invalid enum values, and extra properties.

3
00:00:23.808 --> 00:00:34.779
Boundary tests are especially useful because validators often fail at the edges: maximum lengths, minimum amounts, date limits, nesting depth, and collection size.

4
00:00:34.779 --> 00:00:39.447
Malformed input tests help confirm that the application fails safely.

5
00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:48.861
Send strings where numbers are expected, objects where arrays are expected, null where a value is required, and values that parse ambiguously.

6
00:00:48.861 --> 00:00:57.031
Property-based testing can be useful at a high level because it explores many variations instead of only hand-picked cases.

7
00:00:57.031 --> 00:01:04.033
API contract tests help keep clients, servers, schemas, and documentation aligned as the system evolves.

8
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:06.757
Code review should ask direct questions.

9
00:01:06.757 --> 00:01:09.791
Where does external data enter this function.

10
00:01:09.791 --> 00:01:12.514
Is it typed as unknown before validation.

11
00:01:12.514 --> 00:01:15.315
Does the schema reject unexpected fields.

12
00:01:15.315 --> 00:01:18.427
Are type assertions justified by prior checks.

13
00:01:18.427 --> 00:01:23.562
Are client-side and server-side rules aligned without relying on the client.

14
00:01:23.562 --> 00:01:26.986
Are domain invariants enforced somewhere durable.

15
00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:31.421
Do error messages reveal only what a client needs to fix the request.

16
00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:37.334
Those questions keep review practical instead of turning validation into a style debate.

17
00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:40.057
Logging invalid input requires balance.

18
00:01:40.057 --> 00:01:51.883
Logs should support troubleshooting and abuse detection, but they should not store secrets, session tokens, passwords, full payment data, or unnecessary personal information.

19
00:01:51.883 --> 00:02:02.854
It is often enough to log a request identifier, endpoint, validation failure category, user or tenant context when appropriate, and safe metadata such as field names.

20
00:02:02.854 --> 00:02:10.557
Good tests and careful logs turn validation from a pile of checks into an observable, maintainable security control.
